{% extends '@layout/default.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Doctrine enum implementation in Dotkernel{% endblock %}

{% block page_title %}
    <div class="mb-2 text-uppercase small fw-bold text-primary tracking-wider">How to's</div>
    <h1 class="fw-bold text-dark display-6 mb-0">Doctrine enum implementation in Dotkernel</h1>
    <hr class="mt-4 mb-2 opacity-25">
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The update of <code>doctrine/orm</code> to version 3.2.0 saw the introduction of <strong>EnumType</strong> columns. The enum type was introduced in <strong>PHP 8.1</strong>. This new data type is now implemented in Dotkernel, on both the PHP side and the database side.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Below we will discuss some technical aspects behind this update. You can review the full update in <a href="https://github.com/dotkernel/api/pull/339/files" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">this Dotkernel API Pull Request</a>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><a href="#doctrines-approach"></a>Doctrine's approach</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The update introduces the detection of <code>enumType</code> and <code>options.values</code> from a property with <code>type: Types::ENUM</code>. <a rel="noreferrer noopener" href="https://github.com/doctrine/orm/pull/11666" target="_blank">This PR</a> discusses the update and links to several older relevant issues.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading"><a href="#old-setup"></a>Old setup</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">#
class Card
{
    #
    #
    #
    public int $id;

    #],
    )]
    public Suit $suit;
}</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading"><a href="#new-setup"></a>New setup</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">#
class Card
{
    #
    #
    #
    public int $id;

    #
    public Suit $suit;
}</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Note that the type <code>Types::ENUM</code> part is still required if we want to have an actual <code>enum</code> column in MySQL/MariaDB. We still default to <code>Types::STRING</code> or <code>TYPES::INTEGER</code> for columns types with a PHP enum as this is the more portable solution and the safer default.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><a href="#dotkernels-approach"></a>Dotkernel's approach</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading"><a href="#old-setup-1"></a>Old setup</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Dotkernel uses flags for columns like <code>User-&gt;Status</code>, but we resorted to the simpler <code>string</code> type. The obvious disadvantage is that you can't definitively enforce a set of values for a given column. Sure, the PHP can be set up to only use the agrred upon set of values, but the database is independent from it. If you edit a value manually in the database, any string is accepted.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The issue is the same on the side of the PHP code. If the developer adds a value with a typo, it's supported, but will not work as intended.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The only advantage this setup has is the ability to easily add more values in the value set. This may be seen as a feature, but it invites bugs in the execution.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Our old implementation defined the values like below, for the <code>User</code> entity.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">public const STATUS_PENDING = 'pending';
public const STATUS_ACTIVE  = 'active';
public const STATUSES       = ;</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The column for the ORM was defined like this, as a simple string, with <code>pending</code> as its default value:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">#
protected string $status = self::STATUS_PENDING;</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Obviously, the <code>getStatus</code> and <code>setStatus</code> also work with strings:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">public function getStatus(): string
{
    return $this->status;
}

public function setStatus(string $status): self
{
    $this->status = $status;
}</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading"><a href="#new-setup-1"></a>New setup</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Thanks to the update of <code>doctrine/orm</code> to version 3.2.0, Dotkernel can now have a proper link between the PHP code and database values. Now the link between the PHP code and the database is explicit and enforced.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Any update to the value set must be on both the PHP code and the database.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Let's review how the update affects the <code>User</code> entity.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In the next example, we show how to implement a value set using a custom enum.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>First, we define our custom value set in <code>src/User/src/Enum/UserStatusEnum.php</code>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">namespace Api\User\Enum;

enum UserStatusEnum: string
{
    case Active  = 'active';
    case Pending = 'pending';
}</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>We need to create <code>src/User/src/DBAL/Types/UserStatusEnumType.php</code> to process the new values for the <code>status</code> column.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><code>AbstractEnumType</code> must be extended by any future custom enum type.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">namespace Api\User\DBAL\Types;

use Api\App\DBAL\Types\AbstractEnumType;
use Api\User\Enum\UserStatusEnum;

class UserStatusEnumType extends AbstractEnumType
{
    public const NAME = 'user_status_enum';

    protected function getEnumClass(): string
    {
        return UserStatusEnum::class;
    }

    public function getName(): string
    {
        return self::NAME;
    }
}</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you create your own enum types, make sure to update the <code>NAME</code> constant and the value returned by <code>getEnumClass</code>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Let's register the custom type in <code>config/autoload/doctrine.global.php</code> under the <code>types</code> key:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">'types'         => 
    UserStatusEnumType::NAME => UserStatusEnumType::class,

],</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The filtering is updated in <code>src/User/src/InputFilter/Input/StatusInput.php</code>:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">$this->getFilterChain()
    ->attachByName(StringTrim::class)
    ->attachByName(StripTags::class)
    ->attach(fn($value) => $value === null ? UserStatusEnum::Active : UserStatusEnum::from($value));

$this->getValidatorChain()
    ->attachByName(InArray::class, , true);</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The above ensures that the new <code>UserStatusEnum</code> class is used for the <code>status</code> column updates.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The <code>User</code> entity uses the new <code>UserStatusEnum</code> class.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">#)]
protected UserStatusEnum $status = UserStatusEnum::Pending;</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The <code>status</code> getter and setter are also updated:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">public function getStatus(): UserStatusEnum
{
    return $this->status;
}

public function setStatus(UserStatusEnum $status): self
{
    $this->status = $status;
}</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Dotkernel checks the user status during login in <code>src/User/src/Repository/UserRepository.php</code>. If the user is not activated, the login is rejected.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">if ($clientEntity->getName() === 'frontend' &amp;&amp; $result !== UserStatusEnum::Active) {
    throw new OAuthServerException(Message::USER_NOT_ACTIVATED, 6, 'inactive_user', 401);
}</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A new user is created using the <code>enum</code> type and <code>pending</code> as the default.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">$user = (new User())
    ->setDetail($detail)
    ->setIdentity($data)
    ->usePassword($data)
    ->setStatus($data ?? UserStatusEnum::Pending);</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Note the <code>status</code> column in the migration query which now looks like this:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">$this->addSql('
CREATE TABLE user (
	uuid BINARY(16) NOT NULL,
	identity VARCHAR(191) NOT NULL,
	password VARCHAR(191) NOT NULL,
	status ENUM(\'active\', \'pending\') DEFAULT \'pending\' NOT NULL,
	isDeleted TINYINT(1) NOT NULL,
	hash VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,
	created DATETIME NOT NULL,
	updated DATETIME DEFAULT NULL,
	UNIQUE INDEX UNIQ_8D93D6496A95E9C4 (identity), UNIQUE INDEX UNIQ_8D93D649D1B862B8 (hash),
	PRIMARY KEY(uuid)) DEFAULT CHARACTER SET utf8mb4');</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The difference for the migration query is for the <code>status</code> column, highlighted below:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:enlighter/codeblock {"language":"php"} -->
<pre class="EnlighterJSRAW" data-enlighter-language="php" data-enlighter-theme="" data-enlighter-highlight="" data-enlighter-linenumbers="" data-enlighter-lineoffset="" data-enlighter-title="" data-enlighter-group="">old setup: status VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL
new setup: status ENUM(\'active\', \'pending\') DEFAULT \'pending\' NOT NULL</pre>
<!-- /wp:enlighter/codeblock -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><a href="#conclusions"></a>Conclusions</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The old setup used in the Dotkernel applications worked fine, but the limitations were clear as day. There was:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>No enforcement of the value set.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>No link between the PHP code and the database.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The new setup solves both issues, ensuring more consistent flag management for your classes.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

